# Copy to .env for `npm run db:test`.
#
# This file is only about the test database. The two apps have their own —
# see apps/intimacy/.env.example and apps/two-two-two/.env.example. Expo loads
# those itself, and nothing in this file reaches an app bundle.
#
# Nothing here is a secret. It points at a LOCAL throwaway Postgres that the
# suite creates and drops on every run, so never aim it at a database you care
# about — and never at your Supabase project.
#
# Variables already set in your shell win over this file, so a one-off
# `PGHOST=... npm run db:test` still overrides everything below.

# Where Postgres is listening.
#
# Homebrew (`brew install postgresql@16 && brew services start postgresql@16`)
# listens on a socket in /tmp and names its superuser after your OS user:
PGHOST=/tmp
PGPORT=5432
PGUSER=your-os-username

# For `supabase start` instead, use:
#   PGHOST=127.0.0.1
#   PGPORT=54322
#   PGUSER=postgres
#   PGPASSWORD=postgres

# Only if your cluster requires a password. Homebrew's does not.
# PGPASSWORD=

# The existing database used to issue the CREATE/DROP for the throwaway one.
# Defaults to `postgres`, which both Homebrew and `supabase start` provide, so
# this normally needs no setting.
# PGDATABASE=postgres

# Name of the throwaway database. Dropped and recreated on every run, so it
# must not collide with anything real.
# RLS_TEST_DB=apptest_rls
